-- title:
--   The history $H_{t-1}$ as a $\sigma$-algebra
-- statement:
--   Given actions $x_1,x_2,\dots$ and the model's outcomes $y_s=g(x_s,\theta,w_s)$, the history at the start of period $t\ge1$ is
--
--   $$H_{t-1}=\big((x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_{t-1},y_{t-1})\big),$$
--
--   represented by the $\sigma$-algebra on $\Omega$ generated by the pairs $(x_s,y_s)$, $1\le s<t$. Conditioning on $H_{t-1}$ means conditioning on this $\sigma$-algebra; for $t=1$ it is the trivial $\sigma$-algebra (empty history).
--
--   **Formalization Note** `history g θ w xs t` is the paper's $H_{t-1}$ (no natural-number subtraction). The outcomes are computed from the actions, $\theta$ and the disturbances through $g$, not supplied as free variables.
-- source:
--   Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling, Found. Trends Mach. Learn. 11(1) (2018), doi:10.1561/2200000070, Ch. 2, p. 9 (PDF p. 12) and Ch. 5, p. 27 (PDF p. 30), the history H_{t−1}; used in §8.1.2, (8.4), p. 72 (PDF p. 75)

import Mathlib

namespace ThompsonTutorial.Regret

set_option warn.classDefReducibility false in
/-- **The history `H_{t−1}` (as a σ-algebra).**
Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, *A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling*, Found. Trends Mach.
Learn. 11(1) (2018), Ch. 2, p. 9 (PDF p. 12), and Ch. 5, p. 27 (PDF p. 30):
"the history Ht−1 = ((x1, y1), . . . , (xt−1, yt−1)) of observations"; used in §8.1.2, (8.4),
p. 72 (PDF p. 75).

`history g θ w xs t` is the σ-algebra on `Ω` generated by the action–outcome pairs `(x_s, y_s)` of
the periods `1 ≤ s < t`, where the outcomes are those of the model,
`y_s = g(x_s, θ, w_s)`. It is the information available at the start of period `t`; conditioning
on `H_{t−1}` means conditioning on this σ-algebra.

**Formalization Note.** Periods are 1-based, and `history … t` is the paper's `H_{t−1}` (no
natural-number subtraction is used): `history … 1 = ⊥` is the empty history. The outcomes are not
free variables: they are computed from the actions `xs`, the parameter `θ` and the disturbances
`w` through the known function `g`. -/
def history {Ω Θ X Y W : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] [MeasurableSpace Y]
    (g : X → Θ → W → Y) (θ : Ω → Θ) (w : ℕ → Ω → W) (xs : ℕ → Ω → X) (t : ℕ) :
    MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ s ∈ Finset.Ico 1 t,
    MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω => (xs s ω, g (xs s ω) (θ ω) (w s ω))) inferInstance

end ThompsonTutorial.Regret


